{"chunk_id": "ed5bcd075453", "text": "Ghatotkacha, Bhima\u2019s rakshasa son, is slain by Karna using Indra\u2019s divine dart, but in dying he expands his gigantic body and crushes a vast portion of the Kaurava army, destroying an entire Akshauhini. The Pandavas grieve, yet Krishna unexpectedly rejoices. Arjuna questions this strange reaction, and Krishna reveals the strategic truth: Karna has just spent the one irresistible weapon that was destined for Arjuna. Without his natural armor, earrings, and now the celestial dart, Karna is no longer invincible and can eventually be killed. Krishna explains that many earlier events\u2014the deaths or weakening of powerful warriors like Jarasandha, Ekalavya, and others\u2014were also carefully engineered to protect the Pandavas. Dhritarashtra later realizes the same grim logic: Krishna deliberately forced Karna to waste the fatal weapon on Ghatotkacha, turning the rakshasa\u2019s death into a decisive strategic victory.\nKrishna rejoices after Ghatotkacha\u2019s death, revealing Karna has wasted his invincible dart, ensuring Arjuna\u2019s survival and explaining earlier strategic killings of dangerous foes.\nThemes: strategic sacrifice, fate of Karna, Krishna\u2019s war strategy, grief versus hidden victory, removal of powerful enemies", "vector": [-0.0042076390236616135, -0.009827914647758007, 0.009457938373088837, 0.015027763321995735, 0.016749834641814232, 0.024055182933807373, -0.030647488310933113, -0.013547858223319054, -0.03468359261751175, -0.021243363618850708, 0.02486240491271019, 0.006841197144240141, 0.004157187882810831, -0.001142722088843584, -0.008569995872676373, 0.013520950451493263, 0.040549397468566895, -0.018001027405261993, 0.040145788341760635, -0.03799320012331009, -0.004187458660453558, -0.006161786615848541, -0.0005789287388324738, 0.010951297357678413, -0.024283897131681442, 0.03247718885540962, 0.010170984081923962, -0.029920989647507668, 0.011193463578820229, -0.013978376053273678, -0.0136151267